disp: DISTINCT_UNREPRESENTED_CLAUSE
nature: CHECKER_ACCEPTANCE_GATE (malformed manifest elements are errors)
appl: actor=check-dependencies.cjs (CLI and post-change hook); action=report an error for a PackageReference/ProjectReference/PackageVersion start tag with unclosed/mismatched quotes or an unquoted value, instead of skipping it; trigger=checking a recognised manifest; modality=CHECKER ERROR (CLI exits 1); scope=.csproj and Directory.Packages.props elements; exceptions=not full XML validation; comments/conditions not evaluated; timing=check run; enforcement=static code read (lines 102-106), not executed; loading=script
compared: FG-K0-b104215be5 FG-K0-8aea50ed37 FG-K0-15e0f8cf2b FG-K0-3369be1736
rationale: check-dependencies.cjs:102-106 verified (comment: 'an error, so it is never accepted silently'). Both Claude runs described this fail-closed parsing in coverage ledgers but registered no constraint; the K0 groups cover version placement (FG-K0-b104215be5, FG-K0-8aea50ed37), comment/condition handling (FG-K0-15e0f8cf2b) and when checks run (FG-K0-3369be1736). A malformed element fails the check even without any version or dependency violation, so this is a distinct, designed acceptance gate of the enforcement layer.
scenario: A PackageReference with an unquoted Include and no version: no registered rule is violated, the checker reports 'is not well-formed XML ... fix it so the manifest can be checked'.
conf: MEDIUM
proposal: CHECKER_ERROR | check-dependencies run on a recognised manifest | A PackageReference, ProjectReference or PackageVersion element with malformed attribute quoting is reported as an error (never skipped), so the manifest is not accepted until it can be checked.

